}
```
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Modern output</td>
<td>

```css
@media (hover: hover) {
  .foo:hover {

  }
}
```
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fallback for legacy positive output</td>
<td>

```css
.supports-hover .foo:hover {

}
```
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fallback for legacy negative output</td>
<td>

```css
html:not(.supports-touch) .foo:hover {

}
```
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

These media query features have been **supported in all major browsers since December 2018** ([see MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@media/any-hover#browser_compatibility)). That is over several years ago. I consider a JavaScript-based fallback for such well-established features unnecessary.

